    title: "Pro-Israel Hakeem Jeffries says he will push legislation targeting settler violence",
    summary: "Pro-Israel Hakeem Jeffries says he will push legislation targeting settler violence Pro-Israel US Democratic leader, Hakeem Jeffries, who is expected to become the first Black speaker of the House of Representatives next year, has pledged to advance legislation targeting Israeli settler violence in the occupied West Bank within the first 100 days of the new Congress. In an interview with The New York Times, Jeffries said the West Bank Settler Violence Protection and Prevention Act should be among the early legislative priorities, adding that he expected strong support for it within the House Democratic Caucus. Jeffries also described the devastation inflicted on Palestinian civilians in Gaza as “unacceptable, unsustainable and un-American”, calling for increased humanitarian assistance, medical aid, housing support and access to clean water and air. Asked whether he supported the proposed Block the Bombs and Stop the Settlements bills, which call for restricting arms sale to Israel and broader sanctions on settlement expansion, Jeffries said he had not taken a position. On calls to halt US weapons exports used in regional conflicts, he said the current approach was “not sustainable” and needed to change. More than a million people have signed a petition urging Democrats to reject Hakeem Jeffries as the next Speaker of the US House of Representative. An Economist/YouGov poll published last month found that Jeffries had just a 26 percent approval rating among registered voters. Critics say that one reason Jeffries' popularity has taken a hit is that he is an avid supporter of Israel. The American Israel Public Affairs Committee (Aipac) ranks among his top campaign-financing contributors. Jeffries said he expects strong support for the West Bank Settler Violence Protection and Prevention Act. (AFP)",
    preview: "Pro-Israel Hakeem Jeffries says he will push legislation targeting settler violence Pro-Israel US Democratic leader, Hakeem Jeffries, who is expected to become the first Black speaker of the House of Representatives next year, has pledged to advance legislation targeting Israeli settler violence in the occupied West Bank within the first 100 days of the new Congress.",
    body: "Pro-Israel Hakeem Jeffries says he will push legislation targeting settler violence Pro-Israel US Democratic leader, Hakeem Jeffries, who is expected to become the first Black speaker of the House of Representatives next year, has pledged to advance legislation targeting Israeli settler violence in the occupied West Bank within the first 100 days of the new Congress. In an interview with The New York Times, Jeffries said the West Bank Settler Violence Protection and Prevention Act should be among the early legislative priorities, adding that he expected strong support for it within the House Democratic Caucus.\n\nJeffries also described the devastation inflicted on Palestinian civilians in Gaza as “unacceptable, unsustainable and un-American”, calling for increased humanitarian assistance, medical aid, housing support and access to clean water and air. Asked whether he supported the proposed Block the Bombs and Stop the Settlements bills, which call for restricting arms sale to Israel and broader sanctions on settlement expansion, Jeffries said he had not taken a position.\n\nOn calls to halt US weapons exports used in regional conflicts, he said the current approach was “not sustainable” and needed to change. More than a million people have signed a petition urging Democrats to reject Hakeem Jeffries as the next Speaker of the US House of Representative. An Economist/YouGov poll published last month found that Jeffries had just a 26 percent approval rating among registered voters.\n\nCritics say that one reason Jeffries' popularity has taken a hit is that he is an avid supporter of Israel. The American Israel Public Affairs Committee (Aipac) ranks among his top campaign-financing contributors. Jeffries said he expects strong support for the West Bank Settler Violence Protection and Prevention Act. (AFP)",
