    title: "Japan to expand kōsen education into agriculture",
    summary: "The government is also considering adding fields related to content creation, such as manga and anime, in response to demand.",
    preview: "Japan’s education ministry has compiled measures to strengthen the functions of kōsen technical colleges, featuring plans to expand their educational scope to agriculture and increase financial support for new kōsen colleges. With advances in artificial intelligence and other technologies expected to lead to a future shortage of professionals in science, engineering and digital fields, the ministry aims to encourage the establishment of more kōsen colleges to train engineers.",
    body: "Japan’s education ministry has compiled measures to strengthen the functions of kōsen technical colleges, featuring plans to expand their educational scope to agriculture and increase financial support for new kōsen colleges.\n\nWith advances in artificial intelligence and other technologies expected to lead to a future shortage of professionals in science, engineering and digital fields, the ministry aims to encourage the establishment of more kōsen colleges to train engineers.\n\nKōsen are five-year higher educational institutions that students can enter after graduating from junior high school. According to the ministry, there are a total of 58 national, public and private technical colleges across Japan.\n\nThe current standards for establishing kōsen colleges, which stipulate requirements such as the number of teachers and the size of school grounds, contain no provisions for departments outside the industrial field.\n\nTo expand the scope of kōsen education, the ministry plans to introduce similar provisions for agricultural departments. It is also considering adding fields related to content creation, such as manga and anime, in response to demand.\n\nThe ministry will expand financial support for local governments and other entities seeking to establish new kōsen colleges. It plans to raise the maximum amount of subsidies to ¥4 billion from the current ¥2 billion. Nagano, Aichi and Shiga prefectures are among those taking steps to establish new kōsen colleges.\n\nRegarding the academic title of “associate,” awarded to kōsen college graduates, the ministry is considering treating it as an internationally recognized degree.",
